A rainbow mostly reveals rather than does.

Physically, the droplets refract, internally reflect, and disperse sunlight, sorting wavelengths by angle. So the rainbow is not really an object sitting in space; it is an observer-dependent optical geometry. Move the observer and the rainbow moves with them.

It does have consequences, though. It redirects some light into particular directions, carries information about droplet size and illumination geometry, and can act as a cue to organisms capable of perceiving it. Humans then pile on additional effects: attention, emotion, symbolism, navigation cues, weather inference.

So the clean distinction is:

\text{rainbow as physical process} \rightarrow \text{rearranges light}

\text{rainbow as percept} \rightarrow \text{reveals hidden spectral structure}

\text{rainbow as signal} \rightarrow \text{can change behavior in an observer}

It is almost a perfect example of something whose main “function” is not to act on the world, but to make structure visible that was already there.